//
// An ofono2mm update writes its new files to the .distrib our diversions
// keep (before 6.10.2026: over the patched files themselves). Without this
// our copies stay made from the old version next to the package's new files,
// and only the next boot puts that right - which is very hard to explain to
// yourself later.
//
// It must be DPkg::Post-Invoke. DPkg::Post-Invoke-Success parses without
// complaint, shows up in "apt-config dump", and is never run: only
// APT::Update has a -Success variant. Tested by reinstalling ofono2mm and
// finding every patch gone afterwards.
//
// Post-Invoke also runs after a failed dpkg run, hence the guards: no
// modemctl, no ofono2mm directory, nothing happens. Never fatal - apt must
// not fail because of us.
//
// --no-restart is deliberate. The ofono2mm package does not restart
// ModemManager either, so after an update the running daemon still holds our
// patched code and behaves correctly; only the next start needs the files on
// disk to be right, and they are by the time this returns. Restarting would
// buy nothing but the new upstream code - and would cost the mobile data
// connection, because NetworkManager does not survive a ModemManager restart
// (see settle_networkmanager in modemctl).
//
// mtk-build --if-stale comes first: when an update of oFono's MTK plugin has
// just landed under our diversion, it rebuilds the fix for the new version
// (network is there - apt just used it). boot then puts the new build in, or,
// if the build failed, the shipped plugin back. The other way round, boot
// would see the outdated build and remove it before anything was rebuilt.
DPkg::Post-Invoke {
    "if [ -x /usr/bin/modemctl ]; then timeout 300 /usr/bin/modemctl mtk-build --quiet --if-stale || true; fi";
    "if [ -x /usr/bin/modemctl ] && [ -d /usr/lib/ofono2mm/ofono2mm ]; then timeout 120 /usr/bin/modemctl boot --quiet --no-restart || true; fi";
};
